    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d542">

                <pb facs="0746" n="734" sameAs="frus:frus1946v08/pg734"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 Control (Korea)/8–3046: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of
                            State to the Political Adviser in Korea</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Langdon</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-08-30">August 30, 1946</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>116. ActSec Acheson’s statement issued press conference Aug 30 follows:</p>
                <p>[Here follows text of statement printed in Department of State <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Bulletin</hi>, September 8, 1946, page 462.]<anchor
                        n="45" xml:id="d542-f1"/></p>
                <p>Before reading the prepared release, ActSec said that he wanted to emphasize two
                    main points in it. One was that we are prepared at any time that the Soviet
                    Government will do so to resume the discussions of the Commission, the purpose
                    of which is to bring about a unified Korea. The second was that we intend to
                    remain in Korea and carry out our duty there until we have achieved our purpose
                    of bringing into being an independent nation. He added that this is an
                    obligation we intend to perform.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Acheson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d542-f1" n="45" xml:id="d542-f1-content">
                <p>See also Acting Secretary Acheson’s answer to a question on Korea at his press
                    conference on October 1, Department of State <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bulletin</hi>, October 13, 1946, p. 670.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
